## 🎯 Overview

This is a **browser-based sandbox** for exploring how a single video feed can be split across
multiple cellular modems (SIM cards), each with its own independent uplink, and reassembled on a
server before being pushed to Twitch (or any RTMP destination).

It is **not** a production encoder. Nothing is compressed, packetised, or sent over a network.
The "SIMs" are booleans; the "frames" are `ImageData` buffers. The point is to make an intuition
about **failure behaviour** visible — something that is surprisingly hard to reason about on paper.

> 💡 **Zero setup.** Open `index.html` in any modern browser. No build step, no dependencies, no
> network access required.

---

## 📶 Why bond multiple SIMs?

A single cellular uplink is fragile: it drops in tunnels, saturates in crowded cells, and dies
when you walk out of coverage. Bonding several SIMs — ideally from different carriers — gives you:

| Benefit | Description |
|---|---|
| 🚀 **Aggregate bandwidth** | More throughput than any single modem can provide |
| 🛡️ **Redundancy** | Losing one link degrades the stream instead of killing it |
| 🌐 **Diverse failure domains** | Three carriers rarely go down at the same moment |

The interesting question is not *whether* to bond, but **how to divide the frame** between the
links — because that decision determines what a dropout actually looks like to the viewer.

---

## 🧩 The two transmission strategies

The visualiser renders **both variants side by side, live, from the same source frame**, so you
can watch a single SIM drop and compare the damage directly.

### Variant A — Quadrant split

Each SIM is assigned a **rectangular region** of the frame (roughly equal in area) and encodes
*only that region* as its own independent JPEG/PNG stream. The server composites the tiles back
together.
┌─────────┬─────────┐
│ SIM 1 │ SIM 2 │
├─────────┼─────────┤
│ SIM 3 │ SIM 4 │
└─────────┴─────────┘

text

| ✅ Pros | ❌ Cons |
|---|---|
| Simple, cheap, easy to reason about | **A single failed SIM blacks out an entire quadrant** |
| Each SIM does less encoding work | Failure is spatially coherent and immediately obvious |

### Variant B — Pixel round-robin

Each SIM still encodes the **whole frame**, but the server only keeps a pseudo-random `1/n` of the
pixels from each stream and discards the rest. Ownership is a **fixed, balanced, shuffled**
assignment so every SIM gets an equal share.
pixel owner map (n = 4): 1 3 2 1 3 2 1 3 … ← scattered, not striped

text

| ✅ Pros | ❌ Cons |
|---|---|
| Losing a SIM leaves **scattered unclear spots**, not dead regions | More encoding work per SIM |
| The eye is far more forgiving of salt-and-pepper noise than a black rectangle | Missing pixels need **reconstruction** to look decent |
| Massively redundant — every SIM *could* reconstruct the full frame alone | — |

### Optional nearest-neighbour fill (Variant B)

When a pixel's owner SIM is offline, the server can iteratively fill it from its closest
surviving neighbours (a few passes of a simple diffusion/inpainting kernel). The result is a
slightly soft, smeared patch instead of a hard black dot.

> Toggle this in the sidebar to see the difference — it is the single biggest factor in how *bad*
> a single-SIM outage actually looks.

---

## 🔌 Total failure & fiber fallback

If **every** SIM goes offline, the stream does **not** end. The server holds a direct fiber
connection to Twitch independently of the SIM array, so it keeps the RTMP session alive and swaps
the picture for a **"We'll be right back"** card until the cellular links recover.
┌──────────┐ ┌───────────┐ ┌──────────┐
│ Camera │───┐ │ Assembly │ │ Twitch │
└──────────┘ │ │ Server │ └──────────┘
├────▶│ │─────────────▶ (SIM path)
┌──────────┐ │ │ │
│ SIM 1…n │───┘ │ │─────────────▶ (fiber backup)
└──────────┘ └───────────┘

text

This mirrors how real bonded-streaming appliances are deployed: the cellular array is the *bonus*
bandwidth and the *out-of-studio* path, **not** the only path.

> ℹ️ The viewer never sees a "stream offline" screen — they see a hold card, and the stream's
> uptime stat is unaffected. The visualiser switches to this fallback automatically when all SIMs
> are toggled off.

---

## 🎛️ Using the visualiser

| Control | What it does |
|---|---|
| **SIM cards slider (1–8)** | Sets *n*, the number of modems. Ownership maps and quadrant layouts recompute instantly. |
| **SIM chips** | Click any chip to take that SIM offline/online. The LED turns red and the frame updates immediately. |
| **Variant A / Variant B** | Chooses which composite feeds the broadcast preview. |
| **Nearest-neighbour fill** | Enables/disables pixel reconstruction for Variant B. |
| **Bring all SIMs online** | Restores every link. |
| **Take all SIMs offline** | Triggers the total-failure / fiber fallback path. |
| **Fail a random SIM** | Kills one currently-online SIM — the fastest way to feel the difference between variants. |

---

## 🧪 Things worth trying

- 🔢 Set **n = 8** and fail two SIMs. In Variant A that is **25 % of the frame gone**; in Variant B
  it is 25 % of pixels scattered as noise — and with fill enabled, almost invisible.
- 🎚️ Turn **fill off** and compare. This is the honest cost of round-robin: trading coherent block
  loss for high-frequency noise.
- 🔌 Take **everything offline** and confirm the "We'll be right back" card appears while the
  broadcast frame stays mounted.
- 👀 Watch **n = 2**, where the quadrant split is literally left half / right half — the worst case
  for Variant A.

---

## 📁 Project layout
.
├── index.html ← the entire project: markup, styles, and logic in one file
└── README.md ← you are here

text

Everything is **vanilla HTML/CSS/JS** drawn onto `<canvas>` elements. The "camera" is a
procedurally generated animated scene (gradient sky, orbiting light orbs, city silhouette, HUD
overlay, sensor grain) so the file is **self-contained** and needs no external media.

---

## ⚙️ Internals

<details>
<summary><b>Key functions</b></summary>

<br>

| Function | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `drawSource(t)` | Renders the synthetic camera frame into an offscreen canvas. |
| `buildOwnerMap(n)` | Builds the balanced, shuffled pixel→SIM ownership map for Variant B. |
| `getQuadCells(n)` | Computes the rectangular SIM regions for Variant A. |
| `compositeQuadrant()` | Assembles Variant A's image; paints `NO SIGNAL` on dead regions. |
| `compositeRoundRobin()` | Assembles Variant B's image from surviving pixels. |
| `inpaint()` | Nearest-neighbour diffusion fill for missing pixels in Variant B. |
| `renderBroadcast()` | Copies the selected variant into the Twitch preview. |
| `refreshUI()` | Updates chips, badges, wire animations, and the BRB fallback card. |

</details>

<details>
<summary><b>State model</b></summary>

<br>

```js
const state = {
  n: 4,                              // active SIM count (1–8)
  sims: new Array(8).fill(true),     // per-SIM online flag
  fill: true,                        // nearest-neighbour inpaint on/off
  broadcast: "quadrant",             // "quadrant" | "rr"
};
Changing n, sims, fill, or broadcast and calling refreshUI() is enough to drive the whole
render pipeline on the next animation frame.

</details>
⚠️ What this is not
❌ Not a real encoder or transport. Nothing is compressed, packetised, or sent over a network.

❌ Not a network model. It does not simulate latency, jitter, variable bitrate, or per-link
throttling — only binary up/down.

❌ Not a claim that one variant is strictly better. Variant A is cheaper to encode; Variant B
degrades more gracefully. The right choice depends on your hardware budget, your encoder, and how
much you care about a viewer noticing a dropout.

🧪 Ideas for extending it
□ Add per-SIM bandwidth limits and show the assembler struggling to keep up.
□ Introduce transient failures (a SIM drops for 200 ms then returns) and show recovery.
□ Replace the nearest-neighbour fill with a real edge-aware inpainting pass.
□ Add a third variant: slice-based (horizontal stripes) — the common middle ground.
□ Animate the ownership map reshuffling when *n* changes, so you can see pixels migrate.
□ Log a fake "viewer-perceived quality" score per variant per outage.
📄 License
Do whatever you like with it. It exists to make an intuition visible.

